WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.022
A card reader is about to send one short line to a server.

2
00:00:07.022 --> 00:00:11.716
It's a made-up request: approve 100.00 on the card ending 4242.

3
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:23.180
But before a single byte of it moves, the two machines trade three short messages, full of odd numbers like 244,700,535.

4
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.066
Why three?

5
00:00:24.066 --> 00:00:26.263
And what is a connection, anyway?

6
00:00:26.263 --> 00:00:29.393
There's no wire that belongs to just these two.

7
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:30.963
First, the pieces.

8
00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:40.497
Data travels in packets: small parcels of bytes, addressed to a machine, and to a port, the number of one program on it.

9
00:00:40.497 --> 00:00:45.105
Here the reader uses port 50744, and the server port 9000.

10
00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:48.931
On the way, routers pass the parcels along, for everyone.

11
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:59.240
In UDP and the sockets API, we numbered each message from 0, the other side answered with an ACK, got it, and we resent anything unanswered.

12
00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:01.041
TCP does that work for us.

13
00:01:01.041 --> 00:01:06.708
It's a set of rules followed by each machine's kernel, the core of its operating system.

14
00:01:06.708 --> 00:01:09.605
And it numbers every byte, not every message.

15
00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:13.372
And its ACK means: I have every byte before this one.

16
00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:15.753
ACK 10 means bytes 0 to 9 are here.

17
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.685
So before the first byte travels, both sides must agree where the numbering starts.

18
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:23.485
The obvious answer: always start at 0.

19
00:01:23.485 --> 00:01:25.229
And to be fair, it works.

20
00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:31.648
The reader sends the payment, bytes 0 to 16, the server takes it, and the connection closes.

21
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:35.741
But a copy of that parcel got stuck for a while in a router's queue.

22
00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:42.255
A second later, the reader opens a new connection, on the same two ports, and starts at 0 again.

23
00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:44.299
Now the stuck copy turns up.

24
00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:48.364
It fits the new connection perfectly, so the server takes it.

25
00:01:48.364 --> 00:01:50.496
One tap of a card, two payments.

26
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:51.093
Well...

27
00:01:51.093 --> 00:01:53.258
the customer will have questions.

28
00:01:53.258 --> 00:01:57.523
What we want are numbers an old connection would never have used.

29
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.101
So each side picks its own starting number, a new one for every connection.

30
00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:06.835
Starting at 0 every time is like a door code of 0000.

31
00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:10.039
It's the first thing anyone would try.

32
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:19.050
TCP's rules mix a fast clock with the ports and a secret, so every connection starts somewhere different, and an outsider can't guess where.

33
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:24.221
Guess the numbers, and you could slip your own bytes into somebody else's connection.

34
00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:28.503
Now each side must tell the other its number, and know that it arrived.

35
00:02:28.503 --> 00:02:31.792
You've had this problem on a phone call with a bad line.

36
00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:34.602
One person says: can you hear me?

37
00:02:34.602 --> 00:02:36.997
The other says: yes, I hear you.

38
00:02:36.997 --> 00:02:38.194
Can you hear me?

39
00:02:38.194 --> 00:02:39.989
And the first says: yes.

40
00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:45.813
Each person needs to know their own voice gets through, so each one asks, and each one answers.

41
00:02:45.813 --> 00:02:50.271
That's four lines, but the middle two fit in one breath, so it takes three.

42
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:51.947
TCP works the same way.

43
00:02:51.947 --> 00:02:55.042
Can you hear me is: here's my starting number.

44
00:02:55.042 --> 00:02:56.858
Yes, I hear you, is an ACK.

45
00:02:56.858 --> 00:02:59.280
And the middle message carries both.

46
00:02:59.433 --> 00:03:01.387
First message.

47
00:03:01.387 --> 00:03:08.364
The reader says: my bytes start after 244,700,535.

48
00:03:08.533 --> 00:03:13.026
The server answers: the next byte I expect from you is...

49
00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:15.548
The reader's number ends in 535.

50
00:03:15.548 --> 00:03:16.809
Pause and guess.

51
00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:20.365
536.

52
00:03:20.365 --> 00:03:21.113
One more.

53
00:03:21.113 --> 00:03:29.333
This first message carries no data, yet it uses up one number, so the answer is the start plus one.

54
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:33.027
In the same message, the server adds its own starting number.

55
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.257
Third message.

56
00:03:34.257 --> 00:03:40.073
The reader answers: the next byte I expect from you is the one ending in 691.

57
00:03:40.073 --> 00:03:44.377
Now both sides know both numbers, and the payment can go.

58
00:03:44.533 --> 00:03:47.310
This exchange is the three-way handshake.

59
00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:54.893
Each message is named after its flag, a marker at the front: SYN, short for synchronize, then SYN-ACK, then ACK.

60
00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:58.717
The starting numbers are called initial sequence numbers.

61
00:03:58.717 --> 00:03:59.945
And the connection?

62
00:03:59.945 --> 00:04:02.594
It's these numbers, kept in both kernels.

63
00:04:02.594 --> 00:04:04.468
No wire belongs to these two.

64
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:09.630
The phone picture breaks in one place: a word never turns up a minute late.

65
00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:10.501
A packet can.

66
00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:12.137
Remember the stuck copy?

67
00:04:12.137 --> 00:04:16.121
It carries the old connection's numbers, nowhere near this one's.

68
00:04:16.121 --> 00:04:18.879
It doesn't fit, so the server throws it away.

69
00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:23.224
All this costs one round trip, there and back, before the payment moves.

70
00:04:23.224 --> 00:04:26.227
On the lab network, that's a tenth of a millisecond.

71
00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:32.749
To a bank 70 milliseconds away, that's 70 milliseconds of small talk before every new connection.

72
00:04:32.749 --> 00:04:35.447
So keep connections open, and reuse them.

73
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.301
So, what is a connection?

74
00:04:37.301 --> 00:04:42.675
Two random starting numbers, swapped in three messages, and kept by both sides.

75
00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.073
One quick check.

76
00:04:44.073 --> 00:04:47.735
The payment is 17 bytes, starting at byte 536.

77
00:04:47.735 --> 00:04:50.919
What number does the server's ACK carry?

78
00:04:50.919 --> 00:04:52.192
Pause and think.

79
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:55.848
553.

80
00:04:55.848 --> 00:05:03.456
The bytes are 536 to 552, and the ACK names the next one it expects.
